(_ps4.chapters = _ps4.chapters || []).push(
{ n:114, summary:"The sea looks at Israel arriving out of Egypt and runs away. This compact psalm retells the exodus as cosmic drama: when the house of Jacob left a people of strange language, Judah became the Lord's sanctuary and Israel his dominion. The sea fled, the Jordan was driven back, and the mountains skipped like rams while the little hills bounded like lambs. The poet then turns and interrogates creation itself — what ailed thee, O thou sea? Why did you run, Jordan? Why did you skip, mountains? The answer is not an earthquake but a person: the earth should tremble at the presence of the Lord, at the presence of the God of Jacob, who turned the rock into a standing water and the flint into a fountain of waters.",
  context:"A festival psalm that retells the exodus from Egypt, picturing sea, river, and mountains themselves reacting to the presence of Israel's God.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 114:3", text:"The sea saw it, and fled: Jordan was driven back." },
    { ref:"Psalms 114:7", text:"Tremble, thou earth, at the presence of the Lord, at the presence of the God of Jacob;" },
    { ref:"Psalms 114:8", text:"Which turned the rock into a standing water, the flint into a fountain of waters." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Creation itself responds when the God who saves his people draws near.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"In Psalm 114, what did the mountains do when Israel came out of Egypt?", choices:["Skipped like rams","Melted like wax","Crumbled into dust","Burned with fire"], a:0, why:"The psalm says the mountains skipped like rams and the little hills like lambs." },
    { q:"Why does the psalm say the sea fled and Jordan was driven back?", choices:["A strong east wind blew all night","The presence of the God of Jacob made the earth tremble","Pharaoh's army chased them to the shore","The mountains blocked the water's path"], a:1, why:"The psalm answers its own questions: the earth should tremble at the presence of the Lord, at the presence of the God of Jacob." },
    { q:"The psalm remembers God turning rock into standing water and flint into a fountain. What does that image teach?", choices:["Water is scarce in the wilderness","Israel learned to dig wells","God can supply his people's needs from the hardest places","Rocks in Sinai are full of springs"], a:2, why:"By recalling water from the rock, the psalm shows the Lord providing for Israel in the most unpromising terrain." }
  ] },
{ n:115, summary:"Where is now their God? the nations sneer. The psalm's answer: our God is in the heavens and has done whatsoever he pleased. It then dissects the idols of the heathen — silver and gold, the work of men's hands — with a chilling inventory: mouths that cannot speak, eyes that cannot see, ears that cannot hear, hands that cannot handle, feet that cannot walk. Whoever makes them, and whoever trusts them, becomes like them. Three times the psalm calls for trust — Israel, the house of Aaron, all who fear the LORD — because he is their help and their shield. The Lord will bless small and great, and since the dead go down into silence, the living resolve to bless him from this time forth and for evermore.",
  context:"A psalm answering the taunt of nations who mock that Israel's God cannot be seen, contrasting the living God with lifeless idols of silver and gold.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 115:1", text:"Not unto us, O LORD, not unto us, but unto thy name give glory, for thy mercy, and for thy truth's sake." },
    { ref:"Psalms 115:4", text:"Their idols are silver and gold, the work of men's hands." },
    { ref:"Psalms 115:9", text:"O Israel, trust thou in the LORD: he is their help and their shield." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Trust belongs to the God who acts; idols shape their worshippers into their own lifelessness.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"According to Psalm 115, of what materials are the idols of the nations made?", choices:["Wood and stone","Clay and iron","Bronze and marble","Silver and gold"], a:3, why:"The psalm says their idols are silver and gold, the work of men's hands." },
    { q:"What warning does the psalm attach to those who make and trust idols?", choices:["They become like what they worship — unable to speak, see, hear, or walk","They will be robbed by their neighbors","They will die young like the idols' owners","They must offer sacrifices twice a day"], a:0, why:"They that make them are like unto them, and so is every one that trusteth in them — the psalm's sober principle of resemblance." },
    { q:"Why must the living bless the Lord now, according to the psalm?", choices:["Blessing secures next year's harvest","The dead praise not the LORD, neither any that go down into silence","Idols lose power when sung against","Only priests may bless in the daytime"], a:1, why:"Because the dead go down into silence, the psalm resolves that we will bless the LORD from this time forth and for evermore." }
  ] },
{ n:116, summary:"A man rescued from death asks what to give back. The psalm opens with love: I love the LORD, because he hath heard my voice. Sorrows of death had compassed the speaker, and the pains of hell laid hold on him, until he called on the name of the LORD — gracious, righteous, and merciful — who preserves the simple, brought low though he was. His soul is told to return to its rest: God delivered his soul from death, his eyes from tears, his feet from falling, so he will walk before the LORD in the land of the living. His answer to the question What shall I render? is the cup of salvation, paid vows before all his people, and a sacrifice of thanksgiving in the courts of the LORD's house at Jerusalem.",
  context:"A personal thanksgiving psalm in which a worshipper rescued from near-death declares love for the Lord and pays vows publicly in Jerusalem's temple courts.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 116:12", text:"What shall I render unto the LORD for all his benefits toward me?" },
    { ref:"Psalms 116:13", text:"I will take the cup of salvation, and call upon the name of the LORD." },
    { ref:"Psalms 116:15", text:"Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When the Lord delivers us from trouble, gratitude becomes vows kept, thanksgiving offered, and a life walked before him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What does the psalmist resolve to take in response to all the Lord's benefits?", choices:["The shield of faith","The horns of the altar","The cup of salvation","The water of the well"], a:2, why:"He asks what he shall render unto the LORD and answers, I will take the cup of salvation, and call upon the name of the LORD." },
    { q:"Why does the psalmist say he loves the LORD?", choices:["Because the LORD gives him riches and honor","Because his enemies were destroyed before his eyes","Because the LORD keeps him from all sorrow","Because the LORD has heard his voice and his supplications"], a:3, why:"The opening line states it directly: I love the LORD, because he hath heard my voice and my supplications." },
    { q:"What does 'Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of his saints' teach about God's valuation of his people?", choices:["Their lives and deaths matter deeply to him","Their deaths pay for their sins","Only martyrs receive his attention","Death ends his care for them"], a:0, why:"The verse teaches that the Lord treasures his servants even in death; nothing about their passing is overlooked by him." }
  ] },
{ n:117, summary:"Two verses are all this psalm needs to open Israel's song to the whole world. Every nation and every people is summoned to praise the LORD — the invitation crosses every border. Verse two gives the grounds for so sweeping a call: his merciful kindness is great toward us, and the truth of the LORD endureth for ever. Mercy vast enough to reach outsiders and truth durable enough to outlast generations make universal praise the only fitting answer. The hymn moves from command to cause and back to command, opening and closing with praise ye the LORD, a tiny frame around a worldwide choir.",
  context:"A two-verse hymn calling all nations and peoples to praise the Lord, because his merciful kindness and enduring truth reach beyond Israel.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 117:1", text:"O praise the LORD, all ye nations: praise him, all ye people." },
    { ref:"Psalms 117:2", text:"For his merciful kindness is great toward us: and the truth of the LORD endureth for ever. Praise ye the LORD." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God's mercy and truth are big enough to call every nation to praise him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whom does Psalm 117 summon to praise the LORD?", choices:["Only the priests of Israel","All nations and all peoples","The tribes gathered at Jerusalem","The kings of the earth only"], a:1, why:"The opening line calls on all ye nations and all ye people to praise the LORD." },
    { q:"What two reasons does the psalm give for the whole world's praise?", choices:["Wealth and victory","Threats and warnings","Great merciful kindness and enduring truth","Mountains and rivers"], a:2, why:"Verse two grounds the call in his merciful kindness great toward us and the truth of the LORD that endureth for ever." },
    { q:"What does this psalm imply about who belongs in the worship of Israel's God?", choices:["Praise is reserved for covenant insiders","Outsiders may watch but never sing","Only David's line may praise him","God's mercy invites every nation into praise"], a:3, why:"By addressing all nations and peoples, the psalm treats the Lord's mercy as reaching far beyond Israel." }
  ] },
{ n:118, summary:"Four voices open in turn — the worshipper, Israel, the house of Aaron, all who fear the LORD — each repeating that his mercy endureth for ever. Then comes a rescue story: the speaker called on the LORD in distress and was set in a large place. Because the LORD is on his side, he will not fear what man can do; it is better to trust in the LORD than to put confidence in princes. Nations swarmed around him like bees but were quenched as the fire of thorns, and the LORD became his strength, song, and salvation. Chastened but not given over to death, he reaches the gates of righteousness, where the stone the builders refused has become the head stone of the corner — the LORD's doing, marvellous in our eyes. This is the day which the LORD hath made; save now, and blessed be he that cometh in the name of the LORD.",
  context:"A thanksgiving procession psalm that moves from distress to the gates of the LORD's house, crowned by the image of the rejected stone become the cornerstone.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 118:8", text:"It is better to trust in the LORD than to put confidence in man." },
    { ref:"Psalms 118:22", text:"The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner." },
    { ref:"Psalms 118:24", text:"This is the day which the LORD hath made; we will rejoice and be glad in it." }
  ],
  takeaway:"What others reject, the Lord can make the cornerstone; trust him over every prince.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"In the psalmist's picture of being attacked, what did the encircling enemies swarm like?", choices:["Locusts","Bees","Wolves","Wasps"], a:0, why:"They compassed me about like bees, he says, yet they were quenched as the fire of thorns." },
    { q:"What happened to the stone the builders refused?", choices:["It was carried away as rubble","It became the head stone of the corner","It sank into the foundation pit","It was sold to another city"], a:1, why:"The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner, and the psalm calls this the LORD's doing." },
    { q:"Why does the psalmist refuse to fear, even with nations around him?", choices:["His army outnumbers theirs","The enemies are only bees after all","The LORD is on his side, so what can man do unto him","He has already paid off his attackers"], a:2, why:"The LORD is on my side; I will not fear: what can man do unto me? is the psalm's logic of courage." }
  ] },
{ n:119, summary:"The Psalter's longest psalm is a grand tour of the word of God in every key: 176 verses in twenty-two eight-verse stanzas, nearly every line naming scripture by another term — law, testimonies, precepts, statutes, commandments, judgments, word. The poet seeks God with his whole heart, hides the word in his heart against sin, and asks heaven to open his eyes to wondrous things, calling himself a stranger in the earth. Affliction runs through the poem: princes sat and spoke against him, the proud digged pits, yet the word became his comfort, his songs in the house of his pilgrimage, better than thousands of gold and silver, sweeter than honey, a lamp unto his feet and a light unto his path. Settled in heaven and true from the beginning, the word gives great peace — and the psalm ends in confession: gone astray like a lost sheep, the poet begs, seek thy servant.",
  context:"The Psalter's longest psalm: 176 verses in 22 eight-verse stanzas circling the word of God under many names — law, testimonies, precepts, statutes, and commandments.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 119:11", text:"Thy word have I hid in mine heart, that I might not sin against thee." },
    { ref:"Psalms 119:105", text:"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path." },
    { ref:"Psalms 119:165", text:"Great peace have they which love thy law: and nothing shall offend them." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God's word is a lamp for the path: hidden in the heart, it guides, comforts, and brings peace.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"According to the psalm, what is the word of God to the one walking at night?", choices:["A tower of strength","A hammer that breaks rock","A lantern in the storm","A lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path"], a:3, why:"Verse 105 gives the famous image: Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path." },
    { q:"Why does the psalmist hide God's word in his heart?", choices:["That he might not sin against God","So he can recite it before kings","To win arguments with teachers","To guarantee wealth and long life"], a:0, why:"Thy word have I hid in mine heart, that I might not sin against thee — the stored word is protection against wandering." },
    { q:"The psalm links loving God's law with what promised result?", choices:["Freedom from all affliction","Great peace, so that nothing offends them","Instant understanding of every mystery","Victory over every enemy in battle"], a:1, why:"Great peace have they which love thy law: and nothing shall offend them ties devotion to the word with settled peace." }
  ] },
{ n:120, summary:"Distance from home and lies all around: this psalm is the cry of someone living among people who hate peace. It opens with an answered prayer — in my distress I cried unto the LORD, and he heard me — then names the distress: lying lips and a deceitful tongue. The false tongue is interrogated like a criminal: what shall be given unto thee? The sentence is sharp arrows of the mighty with coals of juniper. The speaker's location is the real wound: sojourning in Mesech and dwelling in the tents of Kedar. His soul has long dwelt with him that hateth peace; the closing line is a one-line biography of conflict: I am for peace, but when I speak, they are for war.",
  context:"A short complaint psalm: a worshipper far from home cries to the Lord for deliverance from lying lips and neighbors who answer peace-talk with war.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 120:1", text:"In my distress I cried unto the LORD, and he heard me." },
    { ref:"Psalms 120:2", text:"Deliver my soul, O LORD, from lying lips, and from a deceitful tongue." },
    { ref:"Psalms 120:7", text:"I am for peace: but when I speak, they are for war." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When you dwell among people who hate peace, the Lord still hears the peacemaker's cry.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What two places does the psalmist name as his unwilling home?", choices:["Babylon and Nineveh","Egypt and Edom","Mesech and the tents of Kedar","Tyre and Sidon"], a:2, why:"Woe is me, that I sojourn in Mesech, that I dwell in the tents of Kedar, he laments." },
    { q:"What punishment does the psalm say a false tongue deserves?", choices:["Silence in the grave","Banishment from the city","Repayment in silver","Sharp arrows of the mighty, with coals of juniper"], a:3, why:"Asked what shall be done to the false tongue, the psalm answers with sharp arrows and burning juniper coals." },
    { q:"The psalmist says, I am for peace: but when I speak, they are for war. What does his example teach?", choices:["Peacemakers may still face hostility, and can take their cause to the Lord","Peace is impossible until enemies apologize","War is the answer to lying lips","Silence is the only response to conflict"], a:0, why:"The psalmist stays committed to peace, prays in distress, and is heard; faithfulness to peace survives a war-loving world." }
  ] },
{ n:121, summary:"A traveler lifts his eyes to the hills — the dangerous horizon of any journey — and asks where help will come from. The answer redirects his gaze upward past the hills to their Maker: my help cometh from the LORD, which made heaven and earth. The rest of the psalm is a watchman's report of divine vigilance: he that keepeth thee will not slumber; the keeper of Israel neither slumbers nor sleeps. The LORD is a shade at the right hand, screening travelers from sun by day and moon by night. The verbs keep and preserve pile up until the blessing covers everything: preserved from evil in soul, in going out and coming in, from this time forth and for evermore.",
  context:"A traveler's blessing psalm that lifts eyes to the hills and finds help in the Lord, who watches over his people without slumbering or sleeping.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 121:1", text:"I will lift up mine eyes unto the hills, from whence cometh my help." },
    { ref:"Psalms 121:2", text:"My help cometh from the LORD, which made heaven and earth." },
    { ref:"Psalms 121:8", text:"The LORD shall preserve thy going out and thy coming in from this time forth, and even for evermore." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Help does not come from the landscape but from the Maker of heaven and earth, who never sleeps on watch.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where does the psalmist look at the start of Psalm 121, and what does he conclude?", choices:["To the temple; God lives there","To the hills; his help comes from the LORD, maker of heaven and earth","To the skies; a storm is coming","To the road; the journey is too hard"], a:1, why:"I will lift up mine eyes unto the hills, from whence cometh my help — and the help comes from the LORD who made heaven and earth." },
    { q:"What does the psalm emphasize by saying the keeper of Israel will neither slumber nor sleep?", choices:["God is too busy for individuals","Travelers must take turns watching","God's protective care never dozes or lapses","Sleep is forbidden to the righteous"], a:2, why:"The unblinking watchman image teaches that God's keeping care is constant, day and night, sun and moon notwithstanding." },
    { q:"What scope of life does the final blessing cover?", choices:["Only temple worship","Only journeys to Jerusalem","Only times of danger","Going out and coming in, from this time forth and for evermore"], a:3, why:"The blessing stretches over all comings and goings, for evermore — the whole of life under God's keeping." }
  ] },
{ n:122, summary:"The invitation lands like good news: Let us go into the house of the LORD. I was glad, the pilgrim says, picturing his feet standing inside Jerusalem's gates. The city is praised for its architecture — builded as a city that is compact together, its neighborhoods knit close — and for its traffic: the tribes go up there, the tribes of the LORD, to give thanks and to sit under the thrones of judgment of the house of David. Then the pilgrim turns intercessor: pray for the peace of Jerusalem; they shall prosper that love thee. Peace within her walls and prosperity within her palaces is wished for the sake of brethren, companions, and the house of the LORD our God, for whose sake the pilgrim will seek the city's good.",
  context:"A pilgrim psalm of rejoicing over going up to Jerusalem, praising its compact unity, its tribes and thrones, and praying for the city's peace.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 122:1", text:"I was glad when they said unto me, Let us go into the house of the LORD." },
    { ref:"Psalms 122:3", text:"Jerusalem is builded as a city that is compact together:" },
    { ref:"Psalms 122:6", text:"Pray for the peace of Jerusalem: they shall prosper that love thee." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Loving God's house turns worship into joy and a city's peace into something worth praying and working for.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"How is Jerusalem described as a city?", choices:["Builded as a city that is compact together","White as a city of marble","Golden as the sun at noon","Desolate and forsaken"], a:0, why:"The psalm pictures Jerusalem as compact together — its people and buildings knit close." },
    { q:"What two things does the psalm say happen when the tribes go up to Jerusalem?", choices:["Trading and banking","Giving thanks to the name of the LORD and judgment from David's thrones","Military training and taxation","Planting and harvest festivals"], a:1, why:"The tribes go up unto the testimony of Israel, to give thanks, for there are set thrones of judgment, the thrones of the house of David." },
    { q:"What does the psalm promise to those who love Jerusalem?", choices:["They shall never die","They shall rule its gates","They shall prosper","They shall write its songs"], a:2, why:"Pray for the peace of Jerusalem: they shall prosper that love thee links love for the city with well-being." }
  ] },
{ n:123, summary:"Eyes up, waiting on a hand: this four-verse psalm fixes its gaze on the Lord who dwells in the heavens, the way servants watch a master's hand and a maiden her mistress's — not staring at nothing, but reading every gesture for the signal of mercy. Until that mercy comes, the watchers hold still. The reason for the plea is spelled out twice: we are exceedingly filled with contempt. The soul is stuffed with the scorning of those that are at ease and the contempt of the proud, so the prayer simply repeats itself: have mercy upon us. The psalm asks for nothing except mercy, but asks for it with the patience of a servant who knows whose hand feeds him.",
  context:"A brief communal prayer that lifts eyes to the Lord in heaven like servants watching a master's hand, pleading for mercy under the contempt of the proud.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 123:1", text:"Unto thee lift I up mine eyes, O thou that dwellest in the heavens." },
    { ref:"Psalms 123:2", text:"Behold, as the eyes of servants look unto the hand of their masters, and as the eyes of a maiden unto the hand of her mistress; so our eyes wait upon the LORD our God, until that he have mercy upon us." },
    { ref:"Psalms 123:3", text:"Have mercy upon us, O LORD, have mercy upon us: for we are exceedingly filled with contempt." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When scorn piles up, servants of the Lord keep watching his hand and waiting for his mercy.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whose eyes does the psalm compare to the worshippers' eyes fixed on the Lord?", choices:["A shepherd's over the flock","A watchman's over the city","A sailor's over the stars","A servant's toward a master's hand and a maiden's toward her mistress"], a:3, why:"The simile runs: as the eyes of servants look unto the hand of their masters... so our eyes wait upon the LORD our God." },
    { q:"How long do the worshippers say they will wait upon the Lord?", choices:["Until he have mercy upon us","Until their enemies apologize","Until morning comes","Until the harvest ends"], a:0, why:"The waiting has one endpoint: until that he have mercy upon us." },
    { q:"What does the psalm teach about responding to contempt from the at-ease and the proud?", choices:["Answer scorn with scorn","Take the contempt to the Lord as a plea for mercy","Flee to another land immediately","Demand justice from the courts"], a:1, why:"Rather than retaliate, the worshippers turn their filled-with-contempt condition into a repeated plea: have mercy upon us." }
  ] },
{ n:124, summary:"One long what-if gives this psalm its momentum. If it had not been the LORD who was on our side — say it twice, and let all Israel say it — the outcome was written: men rising up would have swallowed us quick, alive and whole, when their wrath kindled. Waters would have overwhelmed us; the stream would have gone over our soul; the proud waters would have swept clean over it. Against that drowned past the psalm sets two pictures of rescue: not given as a prey to their teeth, and a bird out of the snare of the fowlers — with the snare itself broken. The conclusion is a one-line creed at the close: our help is in the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth.",
  context:"A community thanksgiving that reasons backward — if the Lord had not been on our side — to credit Israel's escape from overwhelming danger to him alone.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 124:1", text:"If it had not been the LORD who was on our side, now may Israel say;" },
    { ref:"Psalms 124:7", text:"Our soul is escaped as a bird out of the snare of the fowlers: the snare is broken, and we are escaped." },
    { ref:"Psalms 124:8", text:"Our help is in the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Looking back at what could have swallowed us is a way of seeing what the Lord actually did.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What animal picture describes Israel's escape in this psalm?", choices:["A deer escaping hunters","A lamb carried by a shepherd","A bird out of the snare of the fowlers","A fish slipping through a net"], a:2, why:"Our soul is escaped as a bird out of the snare of the fowlers: the snare is broken, and we are escaped." },
    { q:"What disaster does the psalm imagine would have happened without the Lord's help?", choices:["A famine across the land","The temple burned with fire","Being swallowed up quick, overwhelmed like proud waters over the soul","A long exile among strangers"], a:3, why:"The psalm stacks flood images: the waters had overwhelmed us, the stream had gone over our soul, the proud waters had gone over it." },
    { q:"Where does the psalm finally locate Israel's help?", choices:["In the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth","In the strength of their warriors","In the walls of their cities","In alliances with neighboring kings"], a:0, why:"The closing line answers plainly: our help is in the name of the LORD, who made heaven and earth." }
  ] },
{ n:125, summary:"Jerusalem sits ringed by high ground, and the psalm turns geography into theology. Those who trust in the LORD are compared to mount Zion itself: unmovable, abiding for ever. As the mountains are round about Jerusalem, so the LORD is round about his people, from henceforth even for ever — the landscape preaches a siege-proof promise. One caution keeps the promise honest: the rod of the wicked shall not rest upon the lot of the righteous, lest suffering squeeze the righteous into wrongdoing. The psalm then prays simply — do good, O LORD, unto those that be good — and warns that those who turn aside into crooked ways will be led out with the workers of iniquity. The last words balance the whole: but peace shall be upon Israel.",
  context:"A trust psalm that likens believers to unmovable mount Zion and the Lord to the mountains surrounding Jerusalem, ending with peace upon Israel.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 125:1", text:"They that trust in the LORD shall be as mount Zion, which cannot be removed, but abideth for ever." },
    { ref:"Psalms 125:2", text:"As the mountains are round about Jerusalem, so the LORD is round about his people from henceforth even for ever." },
    { ref:"Psalms 125:3", text:"For the rod of the wicked shall not rest upon the lot of the righteous; lest the righteous put forth their hands unto iniquity." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Trusting the Lord plants you like a mountain: surrounded, steady, and promised peace.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"To what does the psalm compare people who trust in the LORD?", choices:["Cedars of Lebanon","Mount Zion, which cannot be removed","Rivers in the desert","Stars in the heavens"], a:1, why:"They that trust in the LORD shall be as mount Zion, which cannot be removed, but abideth for ever." },
    { q:"What does the ring of mountains around Jerusalem illustrate?", choices:["The city's isolation from its neighbors","The difficulty of farming there","The LORD surrounding his people from henceforth even for ever","The need to build higher walls"], a:2, why:"As the mountains are round about Jerusalem, so the LORD is round about his people — geography as a picture of protection." },
    { q:"How does the psalm end?", choices:["With a warning to the nations","With a call to build the temple","With a question about suffering","With the promise that peace shall be upon Israel"], a:3, why:"After warning the crooked, the psalm closes: but peace shall be upon Israel." }
  ] },
{ n:126, summary:"The restoration felt too good to be true. When the LORD turned again the captivity of Zion, we were like them that dream, the psalm says: mouths filled with laughter, tongues with singing. Even the heathen noticed and named the cause — the LORD hath done great things for them — a confession Israel joyfully adopts as its own: whereof we are glad. Then the psalm pivots from memory to prayer: turn again our captivity, O LORD, as the streams in the south, asking the past rescue to repeat itself. The closing couplet turns planting into a parable: they that sow in tears shall reap in joy, and the weeping sower bearing precious seed will doubtless come again with rejoicing, bringing his sheaves with him.",
  context:"A return psalm: Zion remembers the Lord turning back her captivity amid dream-like laughter, then prays for renewed restoration and sows in tears for a joyful harvest.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 126:1", text:"When the LORD turned again the captivity of Zion, we were like them that dream." },
    { ref:"Psalms 126:5", text:"They that sow in tears shall reap in joy." },
    { ref:"Psalms 126:6", text:"He that goeth forth and weepeth, bearing precious seed, shall doubtless come again with rejoicing, bringing his sheaves with him." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Sowing in tears is not wasted labor: God turns weeping seed-time into sheaves of joy.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"When the LORD turned again the captivity of Zion, what did the people feel like?", choices:["Them that dream","Soldiers after battle","Travelers lost at night","Mourners at a funeral"], a:0, why:"We were like them that dream, with mouths filled with laughter and tongues with singing." },
    { q:"What farming picture carries the psalm's final promise?", choices:["A vine that never fails","Those who sow in tears shall reap in joy","Wheat stored in barns forever","A fig tree budding overnight"], a:1, why:"The weeping sower bearing precious seed shall doubtless come again with rejoicing, bringing his sheaves." },
    { q:"What principle does the sower image teach about hard seasons of service?", choices:["Tears should be hidden from others","Only abundant harvests matter","Faithful labor done in sorrow will be repaid with joy","Planting is more important than reaping"], a:2, why:"The psalm promises the weeping planter a sure return with rejoicing — present sorrow does not cancel future joy." }
  ] },
{ n:127, summary:"Two professions and a family fill this short psalm: builders, watchmen, and parents all learn the same lesson. Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain that build it; except the LORD keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain. Anxiety gets the same verdict: it is vain to rise up early, to sit up late, to eat the bread of sorrows, for so he giveth his beloved sleep — God's gift includes rest for people who would otherwise grind all night. Then the argument turns domestic: children are an heritage of the LORD, the fruit of the womb his reward. Like arrows in the hand of a mighty man, they give a father standing; happy is the man whose quiver is full, who speaks unashamed with the enemies in the gate.",
  context:"A wisdom psalm for household life: building, guarding, and raising children all succeed only as the Lord grants them, and he gives his beloved sleep.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 127:1", text:"Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain that build it: except the LORD keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain." },
    { ref:"Psalms 127:3", text:"Lo, children are an heritage of the LORD: and the fruit of the womb is his reward." },
    { ref:"Psalms 127:4", text:"As arrows are in the hand of a mighty man; so are children of the youth." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Work and worry cannot replace the Lord's building and keeping; what he gives — even sleep and children — is the real gain.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What picture does the psalm use for children in a parent's life?", choices:["Garden plants by the table","Lamps on a stand","Arrows in the hand of a mighty man","Stones in a city wall"], a:3, why:"As arrows are in the hand of a mighty man; so are children of the youth — and happy is the man with a full quiver." },
    { q:"What does the psalm say the Lord gives his beloved instead of anxious overwork?", choices:["Sleep","Wealth","Victory","Long life"], a:0, why:"It is vain to rise early and sit up late eating the bread of sorrows: for so he giveth his beloved sleep." },
    { q:"What principle unites the builder, the watchman, and the parent in this psalm?", choices:["Skill matters more than effort","Human effort succeeds only when the Lord builds and keeps","Cities need watchmen more than houses need builders","Families are safer than cities"], a:1, why:"Both halves teach dependence: without the LORD building, keeping, and giving, the work is in vain." }
  ] },
{ n:128, summary:"A blessing you can taste, touch, and count opens here. Blessed is every one that feareth the LORD and walketh in his ways: he will eat the labour of his own hands — work that finally feeds the worker — and it shall be well with him. The psalm then paints the table: a wife like a fruitful vine by the sides of the house, children like olive plants round about the table. The logic is stated plainly — thus shall the man be blessed that feareth the LORD — and the horizon widens from the family table to Zion: blessing out of Zion, the good of Jerusalem all the days of life, then the crowning view of children's children, and peace upon Israel.",
  context:"A household blessing psalm describing the God-fearing life as fruitful work, a flourishing family, and the good of Jerusalem, down to grandchildren and peace upon Israel.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 128:1", text:"Blessed is every one that feareth the LORD; that walketh in his ways." },
    { ref:"Psalms 128:3", text:"Thy wife shall be as a fruitful vine by the sides of thine house: thy children like olive plants round about thy table." },
    { ref:"Psalms 128:6", text:"Yea, thou shalt see thy children's children, and peace upon Israel." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Reverence for God walks into ordinary life: the table, the work, the family, and the city all share his blessing.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"In the psalm's household picture, what is the wife likened to?", choices:["A fruitful vine by the sides of the house","A tower of strength","A garden of spices","A lamp in the window"], a:2, why:"Thy wife shall be as a fruitful vine by the sides of thine house, with children like olive plants around the table." },
    { q:"According to the psalm, who eats the labour of his hands happily?", choices:["The king in his palace","The priest at the altar","The merchant in the market","Every one that feareth the LORD and walketh in his ways"], a:3, why:"The blessing of eating one's own work and being well belongs to the God-fearing walker in God's ways." },
    { q:"Where does the psalm finally locate the man's blessing beyond his own home?", choices:["Out of Zion, seeing the good of Jerusalem and his children's children","In foreign trade and travel","In the praise of his neighbors","In the size of his flocks"], a:0, why:"The LORD shall bless thee out of Zion; thou shalt see the good of Jerusalem and thy children's children, with peace upon Israel." }
  ] },
{ n:129, summary:"Israel speaks here like a field crossed and recrossed by plow blades. Many a time have they afflicted me from my youth, may Israel now say — the line repeats, hammering the history of oppression — yet they have not prevailed against me. The image sharpens into flesh: the plowers plowed upon my back; they made long their furrows. Against every scar stands one sentence of theology: the LORD is righteous; he hath cut asunder the cords of the wicked. The psalm then prays that Zion's haters be confounded and turned back, and likens them to grass on the housetops — the thin, rooted-nowhere growth that withers before it grows up, that the mower's hand never gathers, that earns no traveler's blessing as he passes.",
  context:"A psalm recalling Israel's long affliction from its youth under oppressors' plows, trusting the righteous Lord who cuts the wicked's cords and confounds Zion's haters.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 129:2", text:"Many a time have they afflicted me from my youth: yet they have not prevailed against me." },
    { ref:"Psalms 129:3", text:"The plowers plowed upon my back: they made long their furrows." },
    { ref:"Psalms 129:6", text:"Let them be as the grass upon the housetops, which withereth afore it groweth up:" }
  ],
  takeaway:"A history of being cut does not get the last word; the righteous Lord severs the cords of the wicked.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What farm image describes the afflictions Israel suffered?", choices:["Grapes trampled in the press","Plowers plowing long furrows on my back","Thorns choking the wheat","A vineyard left unpruned"], a:1, why:"The plowers plowed upon my back: they made long their furrows — oppression drawn as deep cuts across a body." },
    { q:"What is said of the wicked who are like grass on housetops?", choices:["It feeds the flocks all summer","It is harvested and stored","It withers before it grows up, too thin for the mower's hand","It shades the house from the sun"], a:2, why:"Rooftop grass, with no depth of soil, withereth afore it groweth up — the mower fills not his hand with it." },
    { q:"Despite repeated affliction from youth, what does Israel testify?", choices:["That oppression made them stronger than enemies","That the plowers were later punished the same way","That the afflicted should expect nothing","That the enemies have not prevailed against them"], a:3, why:"Many a time... yet they have not prevailed against me — endurance under the righteous Lord's defense." }
  ] },
{ n:130, summary:"The prayer goes up from the bottom. Out of the depths have I cried unto thee, O LORD — and the plea asks for attentive ears. Then the psalm faces the real depth: not circumstances but sin. If thou, LORD, shouldest mark iniquities, who shall stand? The turn is dramatic: but there is forgiveness with thee, that thou mayest be feared — forgiveness producing reverence, not carelessness. From confession the psalm moves to waiting: my soul doth wait, and in his word do I hope, more than watchmen straining for the morning, an image repeated for emphasis. The solo prayer widens into a national sermon: let Israel hope in the LORD, for with him is mercy and plenteous redemption, and he shall redeem Israel from all his iniquities.",
  context:"A penitential prayer from the depths asking for forgiven sin, waiting on the Lord more than watchmen wait for morning, and promising Israel redemption from iniquity.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 130:1", text:"Out of the depths have I cried unto thee, O LORD." },
    { ref:"Psalms 130:3", text:"If thou, LORD, shouldest mark iniquities, O Lord, who shall stand?" },
    { ref:"Psalms 130:7", text:"Let Israel hope in the LORD: for with the LORD there is mercy, and with him is plenteous redemption." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Forgiveness is the ground of reverence: from the depths, confession becomes hope in the Lord's plenteous redemption.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"From where does the psalmist say he cries to the LORD?", choices:["Out of the depths","From the housetops","Out of the pit of lions","From a ship in a storm"], a:0, why:"Out of the depths have I cried unto thee, O LORD — the psalm's famous opening." },
    { q:"Why does the psalm say forgiveness exists with the Lord?", choices:["So that sin costs nothing","That thou mayest be feared — forgiveness awakens reverence","Because sacrifices pay for it","So Israel can ignore the law"], a:1, why:"But there is forgiveness with thee, that thou mayest be feared: pardon leads to reverent awe, not carelessness." },
    { q:"What does the watchman image teach about hoping in the Lord?", choices:["Watchmen sleep through the night","The morning is uncertain","Hope waits eagerly and confidently for something sure to arrive","Night fears are always imaginary"], a:2, why:"My soul waiteth for the Lord more than they that watch for the morning — repeated twice — pictures hope as certain of dawn." }
  ] },
{ n:131, summary:"Three verses carry a whole curriculum in humility. The speaker disclaims ambition twice over: my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty, and he refuses to exercise himself in great matters or in things too high for him. The self-portrait is one of scripture's gentlest: surely I have behaved and quieted myself, as a child that is weaned of his mother. A weaned child no longer fusses for milk but rests settled and content — so the soul, past its clamoring, lies calm without demanding answers or achievements. Out of that quiet comes the psalm's single request, addressed not to God for himself but to Israel at large: let Israel hope in the LORD from henceforth and for ever.",
  context:"A three-verse psalm of quiet humility: a heart not haughty, a soul calmed like a weaned child, and a call for Israel to hope in the Lord for ever.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 131:1", text:"LORD, my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty: neither do I exercise myself in great matters, or in things too high for me." },
    { ref:"Psalms 131:2", text:"Surely I have behaved and quieted myself, as a child that is weaned of his mother: my soul is even as a weaned child." },
    { ref:"Psalms 131:3", text:"Let Israel hope in the LORD from henceforth and for ever." }
  ],
  takeaway:"A soul weaned from grasping rests content, and humility becomes hope in the Lord.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What picture does the psalmist use for his quieted soul?", choices:["A still lake","A weaned child with its mother","A sleeping shepherd","A dove in the clefts"], a:3, why:"Surely I have behaved and quieted myself, as a child that is weaned of his mother: my soul is even as a weaned child." },
    { q:"What does the psalmist refuse to occupy himself with?", choices:["Great matters and things too high for him","The daily care of his flocks","The songs of the temple","The judgments of the courts"], a:0, why:"He does not exercise himself in great matters, or in things too high for me — humble boundaries for the mind." },
    { q:"What does the psalm recommend as the fitting posture for Israel?", choices:["Pursuing great knowledge","Hoping in the LORD from henceforth and for ever","Seeking honor among nations","Building greater storehouses"], a:1, why:"The psalm ends with the one ambition it endorses: let Israel hope in the LORD from henceforth and for ever." }
  ] },
{ n:132, summary:"David's vow drives the first half: he swore he would not come into his own tabernacle, nor go up into his bed, nor give sleep to his eyes, until he found a place for the LORD, a habitation for the mighty God of Jacob. Word of the place echoes through memory — heard of at Ephratah, found in the fields of the wood — and the worshippers resolve to enter his tabernacles and worship at his footstool: arise, O LORD, into thy rest, thou and the ark of thy strength. Then the psalm turns oracle: the LORD has sworn to David that of the fruit of his body he will set someone on his throne, a covenant conditioned on keeping covenant. Because the LORD hath chosen Zion for his habitation, he promises bread for her poor, salvation-clothed priests, the horn of David made to bud, a lamp for his anointed, and a flourishing crown.",
  context:"A procession psalm recalling David's vow to find the Lord a dwelling, the ark's rest, God's sworn covenant with David's line, and his chosen rest at Zion.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 132:8", text:"Arise, O LORD, into thy rest; thou, and the ark of thy strength." },
    { ref:"Psalms 132:11", text:"The LORD hath sworn in truth unto David; he will not turn from it; Of the fruit of thy body will I set upon thy throne." },
    { ref:"Psalms 132:13", text:"For the LORD hath chosen Zion; he hath desired it for his habitation." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God keeps his sworn covenant: he dwells where he chooses and raises up David's line as he promised.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did David vow he would not do until he found a place for the LORD?", choices:["Eat bread or drink wine","Go up to Jerusalem","Come into his own house or give sleep to his eyes","Play the harp again"], a:2, why:"David swore he would not enter his tabernacle or bed, nor give sleep to his eyes, until he found a habitation for the mighty God of Jacob." },
    { q:"What does the psalm say the LORD chose for his habitation?", choices:["The tabernacle in the wilderness","Mount Sinai","The fields of Ephratah","Zion, his rest for ever"], a:3, why:"For the LORD hath chosen Zion; he hath desired it for his habitation. This is my rest for ever: here will I dwell." },
    { q:"What promise does God attach to David's descendants keeping his covenant?", choices:["Their children shall sit upon the throne for evermore","Their enemies will convert","Their kingdom will grow rich","Their priests will never die"], a:0, why:"If thy children keep my covenant... their children shall also sit upon thy throne for evermore." }
  ] },
{ n:133, summary:"Behold, the psalm says — stop and look at something rare: brethren dwelling together in unity. It is pronounced good and pleasant, and then measured twice. First as anointing oil: like the precious ointment poured on Aaron's head at his consecration, running down his beard, down to the skirts of his garments — unity spreads from the top until it covers the whole body. Then as mountain dew: the dew of Hermon settling on the mountains of Zion, moisture from a high peak refreshing the hill country and making barren places live. The final line gives both pictures one destination: for there the LORD commanded the blessing, even life for evermore. Unity is not just pleasant company; it is the place where God commands life.",
  context:"A three-verse wisdom song praising brotherly unity through two images: consecrating oil running down Aaron's robes and Hermon's dew falling on Zion, where the Lord commands blessing.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 133:1", text:"Behold, how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity!" },
    { ref:"Psalms 133:2", text:"It is like the precious ointment upon the head, that ran down upon the beard, even Aaron's beard: that went down to the skirts of his garments;" },
    { ref:"Psalms 133:3", text:"As the dew of Hermon, and as the dew that descended upon the mountains of Zion: for there the LORD commanded the blessing, even life for evermore." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Unity among God's people is sacred ground: there the Lord commands the blessing of life for evermore.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What two pictures illustrate unity in Psalm 133?", choices:["A shepherd and his sheep","Anointing oil on Aaron and the dew of Hermon","A harvest and a wedding feast","A city wall and a river"], a:1, why:"Unity is likened to precious ointment running down Aaron's beard and to the dew of Hermon descending on Zion's mountains." },
    { q:"What did the ointment on Aaron's head do, according to the psalm?", choices:["Stayed only on his head","Was wiped off and stored","Ran down to the beard and the skirts of his garments","Was shared among the priests"], a:2, why:"The oil flows downward, covering the whole figure — a picture of blessing spreading through the whole body of brethren." },
    { q:"What does the psalm say the Lord commanded at Zion?", choices:["The building of the temple","The census of the tribes","The keeping of festivals","The blessing, even life for evermore"], a:3, why:"For there the LORD commanded the blessing, even life for evermore — unity is the site of God's life-giving blessing." }
  ] },
{ n:134, summary:"A night-shift liturgy in three sentences. Standing in the house of the LORD by night — the temple servants who kept watch when the courts were dark — are called to lift up their hands in the sanctuary and bless the LORD. Praise, the psalm insists, does not close at sundown; those on duty in the dark owe God their blessing as much as daytime worshippers do. Then the direction reverses: the servants bless the Lord, and the Lord blesses the servants. The final verse turns blessing back on the blessers — the LORD that made heaven and earth bless thee out of Zion — so that a circuit of blessing completes itself between Maker and ministers in the night.",
  context:"A brief evening blessing: temple servants standing watch by night are called to bless the Lord with lifted hands, and the Lord answers with blessing from Zion.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 134:1", text:"Behold, bless ye the LORD, all ye servants of the LORD, which by night stand in the house of the LORD." },
    { ref:"Psalms 134:2", text:"Lift up your hands in the sanctuary, and bless the LORD." },
    { ref:"Psalms 134:3", text:"The LORD that made heaven and earth bless thee out of Zion." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord deserves blessing in the night seasons, and he blesses those who bless him.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"When do the servants in this psalm stand in the house of the LORD?", choices:["By night","At dawn","Only on Sabbaths","During harvest"], a:0, why:"Behold, bless ye the LORD, all ye servants of the LORD, which by night stand in the house of the LORD." },
    { q:"What gesture does the psalm pair with blessing the Lord in the sanctuary?", choices:["Kneeling in silence","Lifting up your hands","Burning incense","Clapping in rhythm"], a:1, why:"Lift up your hands in the sanctuary, and bless the LORD — bodily praise from those on night duty." },
    { q:"What does the closing blessing teach about the direction of worship?", choices:["Worship flows only upward to God","Blessing returns: those who bless the Lord are blessed out of Zion","Only priests receive blessings","Night worship earns double reward"], a:2, why:"After the servants bless the LORD, the final verse blesses them back — the LORD that made heaven and earth bless thee out of Zion." }
  ] },
{ n:135, summary:"This psalm gathers the Psalter's standing themes into one chorus. It opens by summoning the servants who stand in the LORD's house and courts: praise the LORD, for the LORD is good; sing praises unto his name, for it is pleasant. The reasons stack up in order: the LORD chose Jacob for his peculiar treasure; he is great, above all gods, doing whatsoever he pleases in heaven, earth, seas, and deep places; he sends vapours, lightning, and wind from his treasuries. History joins nature: the firstborn of Egypt smitten, tokens and wonders on Pharaoh, Sihon and Og and Canaan's kingdoms conquered, their land given as an heritage to Israel. Then the psalm repeats the idol satire of silver-and-gold statues with mouths that speak not, before passing the blessing down the ranks — house of Israel, house of Aaron, house of Levi, all who fear the LORD — because he dwelleth at Jerusalem.",
  context:"A temple praise psalm rehearsing the Lord's greatness above all gods, his wonders in Egypt and Canaan, the emptiness of idols, and blessings called down on every rank of servants.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 135:3", text:"Praise the LORD; for the LORD is good: sing praises unto his name; for it is pleasant." },
    { ref:"Psalms 135:4", text:"For the LORD hath chosen Jacob unto himself, and Israel for his peculiar treasure." },
    { ref:"Psalms 135:13", text:"Thy name, O LORD, endureth for ever; and thy memorial, O LORD, throughout all generations." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord who chose his people and keeps his name for ever outranks every idol and every so-called god.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whom does the psalm name as kings the LORD smote to give Israel their land?", choices:["Pharaoh and Nebuchadnezzar","Sihon king of the Amorites and Og king of Bashan","Agag and Haman","Jabin and Sisera"], a:3, why:"The psalm lists Sihon king of the Amorites, Og king of Bashan, and all the kingdoms of Canaan, whose land became Israel's heritage." },
    { q:"What does the psalm say the LORD does with wind?", choices:["Brings the wind out of his treasuries","Stores wind in caves under the sea","Sells the wind to merchants","Sends wind only in wartime"], a:0, why:"He bringeth the wind out of his treasuries, alongside vapours ascending and lightnings made for rain — weather as his inventory." },
    { q:"What is the verdict on idols that have mouths but speak not?", choices:["They are mysterious powers","Their makers and trusters become like them — lifeless","They should be respected as art","They guard the homes that own them"], a:1, why:"They that make them are like unto them: so is every one that trusteth in them — trust shapes the truster." }
  ] },
{ n:136, summary:"One refrain carries the whole psalm: for his mercy endureth for ever. It answers all twenty-six calls to give thanks, giving the hymn a call-and-response shape — one line naming God's deeds, the same answer returning every time. The catalog moves from creation to conquest to table: thanks to the God of gods and Lord of lords, who alone doeth great wonders, who by wisdom made the heavens and stretched out the earth above the waters, who made the sun to rule the day and the moon and stars to rule the night. Then the exodus: Egypt's firstborn smitten, Israel brought out with a strong hand and a stretched out arm, the Red sea divided, Pharaoh's host overthrown, the wilderness march, Sihon and Og defeated, land given as an heritage. The ending comes home: he remembered us in our low estate, redeemed us from our enemies, and gives food to all flesh.",
  context:"A great thanksgiving hymn in call-and-response form: every line naming one of God's mighty acts from creation to the exodus is answered by the same refrain of enduring mercy.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 136:1", text:"O give thanks unto the LORD; for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever." },
    { ref:"Psalms 136:5", text:"To him that by wisdom made the heavens: for his mercy endureth for ever." },
    { ref:"Psalms 136:23", text:"Who remembered us in our low estate: for his mercy endureth for ever:" }
  ],
  takeaway:"Every act of God — creating, delivering, feeding — is an installment of a mercy that never runs out.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What refrain follows every line of this psalm?", choices:["Praise ye the LORD","His steadfast love lasts a night","For his mercy endureth for ever","Holy, holy, holy"], a:2, why:"Each of the psalm's calls and acts ends with the same answer: for his mercy endureth for ever." },
    { q:"How does the psalm describe the way Israel was brought out of Egypt?", choices:["Quietly and by night","With wealth borrowed from neighbors","By a swift flight through the desert","With a strong hand, and with a stretched out arm"], a:3, why:"The exodus is credited to God's strong hand and stretched out arm, which divided the Red sea and overthrew Pharaoh's host." },
    { q:"The psalm moves from making heavens and great lights to giving food to all flesh. What does the sweep teach?", choices:["The same enduring mercy governs creation and daily bread","Creation matters more than history","Only Israel receives God's food","God's power fades over time"], a:0, why:"One refrain binds cosmic acts and everyday provision together: mercy endures through every scale of God's work." }
  ] },
{ n:137, summary:"Captivity begins with an inability to sing. By the rivers of Babylon the exiles sat down and wept when they remembered Zion, hanging their harps on the willows. Their captors demanded entertainment — sing us one of the songs of Zion — and the question that answers them is piercing: how shall we sing the LORD's song in a strange land? Two oaths follow, binding memory to the body itself: if I forget thee, O Jerusalem, let my right hand forget her cunning; if I do not remember thee, let my tongue cleave to the roof of my mouth — Jerusalem above my chief joy. The psalm then turns from grief to account-settling: it asks the LORD to remember Edom, who cheered Jerusalem's demolition — rase it, rase it — and ends with hard words wishing retribution on daughter Babylon, who art to be destroyed.",
  context:"An exile's psalm from the rivers of Babylon: weeping exiles refuse to sing Zion's songs on demand, swear loyalty to Jerusalem, and ask the Lord to remember Edom's cruelty.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 137:1", text:"By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat down, yea, we wept, when we remembered Zion." },
    { ref:"Psalms 137:4", text:"How shall we sing the LORD's song in a strange land?" },
    { ref:"Psalms 137:5", text:"If I forget thee, O Jerusalem, let my right hand forget her cunning." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Loyalty to God's city and song can survive even exile — memory holds on when singing is impossible.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did the exiles do with their harps in Babylon?", choices:["Played them for their captors","Hanged them upon the willows","Buried them beneath the rivers","Sold them in the market"], a:1, why:"We hanged our harps upon the willows in the midst thereof — grief silenced their music." },
    { q:"What self-curse shows how seriously the singer holds on to Jerusalem?", choices:["Let my right hand forget her cunning and my tongue cleave to the roof of my mouth","Let my enemies triumph over me","Let my name be blotted out of the book","Let my children wander for ever"], a:2, why:"The singer would rather lose the skill of his hand and the use of his tongue than forget Jerusalem above his chief joy." },
    { q:"The psalmist entrusts the memory of Edom's brutality to whom?", choices:["To the exiles' own revenge","To future generations of singers","To the kings of the Medes","To the LORD, saying Remember"], a:3, why:"Remember, O LORD, the children of Edom in the day of Jerusalem — justice is left in the Lord's hands." }
  ] },
{ n:138, summary:"The praise here is whole-hearted and public: I will praise thee with my whole heart; before the gods will I sing praise unto thee. Worshipping toward the holy temple, the singer thanks God's lovingkindness and truth, for thou hast magnified thy word above all thy name. The psalm remembers an answered cry: in the day when I cried thou answeredst me, and strengthenedst me with strength in my soul. That experience scales up to a forecast — all the kings of the earth shall praise the LORD when they hear the words of his mouth and sing in the ways of the LORD. The theology pivots on one contrast: though the LORD be high, yet hath he respect unto the lowly, but the proud he knoweth afar off. So the troubled singer stays confident: the LORD will perfect that which concerneth me, and his right hand shall save him.",
  context:"A personal thanksgiving psalm: the singer praises God wholeheartedly before the gods, recalls answered prayer, and trusts the high Lord who respects the lowly to perfect what concerns him.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 138:1", text:"I will praise thee with my whole heart: before the gods will I sing praise unto thee." },
    { ref:"Psalms 138:6", text:"Though the LORD be high, yet hath he respect unto the lowly: but the proud he knoweth afar off." },
    { ref:"Psalms 138:8", text:"The LORD will perfect that which concerneth me: thy mercy, O LORD, endureth for ever: forsake not the works of thine own hands." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The high God stoops to the lowly: he hears cries, strengthens souls, and finishes what he starts in us.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"According to this psalm, what has God magnified above all his name?", choices:["His word","His temple","His throne","His angels"], a:0, why:"For thou hast magnified thy word above all thy name, the singer says while praising God's lovingkindness and truth." },
    { q:"What contrast stands at the heart of the psalm?", choices:["Day against night","The high LORD respecting the lowly while knowing the proud afar off","Kings against priests","Silver against gold"], a:1, why:"Though the LORD be high, yet hath he respect unto the lowly: but the proud he knoweth afar off." },
    { q:"What confidence does the closing verse express?", choices:["That enemies will vanish without conflict","That kings will send gifts","That the LORD will perfect that which concerneth me","That trouble will never come again"], a:2, why:"The LORD will perfect that which concerneth me — he completes the work in the lives of those he has made." }
  ] },
{ n:139, summary:"Someone is reading over your shoulder — and the psalm calls that comfort. O LORD, thou hast searched me and known me: my downsitting and mine uprising, my thought afar off, every path and lying down, every word before it is spoken. Such knowledge is too wonderful; the poet cannot attain it, and neither can he escape it. Heaven, hell, the wings of the morning, the uttermost parts of the sea, even darkness turned to day — everywhere thy hand leads. The search moves inward to the womb: thou hast covered me in my mother's womb; I am fearfully and wonderfully made, my unformed substance written in God's book. God's thoughts toward the poet outnumber the sand. A hard turn takes up the wicked and bloody men, hated with perfect hatred, before the psalm circles home: search me, O God, and know my heart — lead me in the way everlasting.",
  context:"A meditation on God's all-knowing presence: searched and surrounded everywhere from heaven to the womb's making, the poet invites further searching — know my heart, lead me in the way everlasting.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 139:7", text:"Whither shall I go from thy spirit? or whither shall I flee from thy presence?" },
    { ref:"Psalms 139:14", text:"I will praise thee; for I am fearfully and wonderfully made: marvellous are thy works; and that my soul knoweth right well." },
    { ref:"Psalms 139:23", text:"Search me, O God, and know my heart: try me, and know my thoughts:" }
  ],
  takeaway:"You are fully known — thoughts, words, and making — and being known by God is an invitation, not a threat.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Where does the psalmist say he cannot flee from God's presence?", choices:["Only from the battlefield","Only from the temple courts","Nowhere except the darkness of night","Not even in heaven, hell, or the farthest sea"], a:3, why:"If I ascend up into heaven, thou art there: if I make my bed in hell, behold, thou art there — even wings of the morning and the uttermost sea cannot escape him." },
    { q:"What does the psalm say about the darkness?", choices:["The darkness and the light are both alike to God","Darkness hides deeds from heaven","Night belongs to the wicked","Darkness should be feared at all times"], a:0, why:"The darkness hideth not from thee; but the night shineth as the day: the darkness and the light are both alike to thee." },
    { q:"How does the psalmist respond to God's perfect knowledge of him?", choices:["By trying to hide his thoughts","By asking God to search him and lead him in the way everlasting","By promising to earn God's favor","By refusing to speak another word"], a:1, why:"Search me, O God, and know my heart... and see if there be any wicked way in me — full knowledge becomes full surrender." }
  ] },
{ n:140, summary:"Two faces haunt this prayer: the evil man and the violent man. They imagine mischiefs and gather for war; they have sharpened their tongues like a serpent, with adders' poison under their lips. The psalmist sees the traps — a hidden snare, cords, a net spread by the wayside, gins set for him — and prays twice for keeping. His answer to the danger is a declaration: I said unto the LORD, Thou art my God. He recalls that God covered his head in the day of battle, and asks that the wicked's own devices be frustrated: let the mischief of their own lips cover them; burning coals fall; deep pits hold them; the evil speaker find no footing in the earth. The psalm steadies on certainty: the LORD will maintain the cause of the afflicted, and the right of the poor, until the upright dwell in his presence.",
  context:"A plea for protection from violent plotters whose tongues carry serpent poison, resting on the confidence that the Lord maintains the cause of the afflicted and the right of the poor.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 140:1", text:"Deliver me, O LORD, from the evil man: preserve me from the violent man;" },
    { ref:"Psalms 140:3", text:"They have sharpened their tongues like a serpent; adders' poison is under their lips." },
    { ref:"Psalms 140:12", text:"I know that the LORD will maintain the cause of the afflicted, and the right of the poor." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God defends the afflicted: traps set by the violent cannot overturn the cause he maintains.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What weapon does the psalm attribute to the evildoers' speech?", choices:["Stones from a sling","Arrows of false oaths","Tongues sharpened like a serpent, with adders' poison","Rods of iron"], a:2, why:"They have sharpened their tongues like a serpent; adders' poison is under their lips." },
    { q:"What has the LORD done for the psalmist in past danger?", choices:["Given him a fortress of stone","Hidden him in a cave","Sent angels to feed him","Covered his head in the day of battle"], a:3, why:"O GOD the Lord, the strength of my salvation, thou hast covered my head in the day of battle." },
    { q:"What conviction anchors the psalm amid plots and snares?", choices:["The LORD will maintain the cause of the afflicted, and the right of the poor","Violent men eventually repent","The righteous should flee the country","Courts will deliver perfect justice"], a:0, why:"I know that the LORD will maintain the cause of the afflicted — the certainty behind every plea in the psalm." }
  ] },
{ n:141, summary:"Evening worship supplies the psalm's first picture: let my prayer be set forth before thee as incense, and the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice. From there the prayer guards its own holiness: set a watch, O LORD, before my mouth; keep the door of my lips — and keep the heart from evil company and their dainties. Correction is welcomed rather than resented: let the righteous smite me; it shall be a kindness, and his reproof an excellent oil that will not break the head. Amid danger the psalmist keeps looking up — our bones are scattered at the grave's mouth, yet mine eyes are unto thee, O GOD the Lord. He asks only to be kept from the snares while the wicked fall into their own nets.",
  context:"A prayer at evening time asking that prayer rise like incense, that the Lord guard the speaker's lips and heart from evildoers' snares, and that righteous reproof be welcomed as kindness.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 141:2", text:"Let my prayer be set forth before thee as incense; and the lifting up of my hands as the evening sacrifice." },
    { ref:"Psalms 141:3", text:"Set a watch, O LORD, before my mouth; keep the door of my lips." },
    { ref:"Psalms 141:10", text:"Let the wicked fall into their own nets, whilst that I withal escape." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Guarded speech and a teachable heart keep prayer rising like incense, even among traps.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What does the psalmist ask God to set before his mouth?", choices:["A sword of defense","A watch","A table of bread","A lamp of light"], a:1, why:"Set a watch, O LORD, before my mouth; keep the door of my lips." },
    { q:"How does the psalmist feel about being rebuked by the righteous?", choices:["He rejects it as pride","He fears it will break his head","He counts the smiting a kindness and the reproof an excellent oil","He demands it stop immediately"], a:2, why:"Let the righteous smite me; it shall be a kindness — reproof from the good is anointing, not injury." },
    { q:"What outcome does the psalmist expect from the nets laid for him?", choices:["That he will learn to snare others","That the nets will become his home","That new nets will replace the old","That the wicked will fall into their own nets while he escapes"], a:3, why:"Let the wicked fall into their own nets, whilst that I withal escape — evil's traps rebound on their makers." }
  ] },
{ n:142, summary:"Alone, overwhelmed, and out loud: this is a prayer for the person at the end of himself. I poured out my complaint before him; I shewed before him my trouble — nothing held back, the whole grief emptied out before the LORD. Inside, the spirit was overwhelmed; outside, a snare had been laid privily in the path. The loneliest line follows: I looked on my right hand, and beheld, but there was no man that would know me; refuge failed me; no man cared for my soul. Out of that vacancy comes the psalm's confession: thou art my refuge and my portion in the land of the living. The psalm ends asking for the only rescue that matters: bring my soul out of prison, that I may praise thy name, with the righteous compassing him about and bounty promised.",
  context:"A lonely complaint prayer in which the distressed speaker, deserted by others and trapped by persecutors, names the Lord as refuge and portion and asks for his soul to be brought out of prison.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 142:2", text:"I poured out my complaint before him; I shewed before him my trouble." },
    { ref:"Psalms 142:4", text:"I looked on my right hand, and beheld, but there was no man that would know me: refuge failed me; no man cared for my soul." },
    { ref:"Psalms 142:7", text:"Bring my soul out of prison, that I may praise thy name: the righteous shall compass me about; for thou shalt deal bountifully with me." }
  ],
  takeaway:"When no man cares for your soul, the Lord is still refuge and portion — and prayer opens the prison.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What did the psalmist see when he looked on his right hand?", choices:["No man that would know him","An angel standing guard","His enemies retreating","A city of refuge"], a:0, why:"Refuge failed me; no man cared for my soul — the psalm's picture of total abandonment." },
    { q:"What does the psalmist ask the Lord to bring out of prison?", choices:["His family from exile","His soul, so that he may praise the Lord's name","His gold from the treasury","His writings from the cave"], a:1, why:"Bring my soul out of prison, that I may praise thy name: the goal of rescue is renewed worship." },
    { q:"When human support disappears, what does the psalm teach the believer to do?", choices:["Stop praying until friends return","Rely on hidden provisions","Name the Lord as refuge and portion, and pour out the whole complaint to him","Pretend the trouble is smaller than it is"], a:2, why:"The psalm holds honest complaint and confident confession together: I poured out my complaint... thou art my refuge and my portion." }
  ] },
{ n:143, summary:"A distressed prayer that keeps asking to be taught while it asks to be saved. Hear my prayer, O LORD, in thy faithfulness answer me — but, strikingly, and enter not into judgment with thy servant: for in thy sight shall no man living be justified. The enemy has smitten life down to the ground and made it dwell in darkness, as those long dead; the spirit is overwhelmed and the heart desolate. The remedy is memory and thirst: I remember the days of old; I meditate on all thy works, while a parched soul stretches out its hands. The requests narrow to guidance: cause me to hear thy lovingkindness in the morning; cause me to know the way wherein I should walk; teach me to do thy will, for thy spirit is good. Quicken me for thy name's sake, and bring my soul out of trouble.",
  context:"A prayer under enemy persecution that asks for mercy rather than strict judgment, confessing that no one is justified before God, and pleading to be taught the way to walk.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 143:2", text:"And enter not into judgment with thy servant: for in thy sight shall no man living be justified." },
    { ref:"Psalms 143:6", text:"I stretch forth my hands unto thee: my soul thirsteth after thee, as a thirsty land. Selah." },
    { ref:"Psalms 143:10", text:"Teach me to do thy will; for thou art my God: thy spirit is good; lead me into the land of uprightness." }
  ],
  takeaway:"No one stands before God on merit; salvation and guidance both come from his mercy — teach me to do thy will.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What natural image describes the psalmist's longing for God?", choices:["Like a deer by the waterbrooks","Like frost before the sun","Like a harvest waiting for rain","My soul thirsteth after thee, as a thirsty land"], a:3, why:"I stretch forth my hands unto thee: my soul thirsteth after thee, as a thirsty land." },
    { q:"Why does the psalmist ask God not to enter into judgment with him?", choices:["In thy sight shall no man living be justified","He has no time for a trial","Judgment belongs to kings only","His enemies would testify against him"], a:0, why:"The plea admits universal guilt: if God judged strictly, no one could stand — so he pleads faithfulness and righteousness instead." },
    { q:"What does the psalmist want to learn more than rescue itself?", choices:["The number of his days","To do God's will and walk the way God shows","The songs of the temple","The plans of his enemies"], a:1, why:"Teach me to do thy will... cause me to know the way wherein I should walk — guidance is the heart's request." }
  ] },
{ n:144, summary:"A warrior-king opens with trained hands: blessed be the LORD my strength, which teacheth my hands to war — the LORD is his fortress, high tower, deliverer, and shield. Amazement interrupts the arsenal: LORD, what is man, that thou takest knowledge of him? Man is like to vanity; his days are as a shadow that passeth away. Yet the king asks God to intervene in cosmic style — bow thy heavens and come down, touch the mountains, cast forth lightning, shoot out thine arrows — to rid him from great waters and from strange children whose right hand is a right hand of falsehood. Deliverance becomes a new song upon a psaltery and an instrument of ten strings, and then a social vision: sons like plants grown up in their youth, daughters like polished corner stones, garners full, flocks multiplied, no breaking in and no complaining in our streets. Happy is that people, whose God is the LORD.",
  context:"A royal psalm of battle and blessing: the Lord trains the king's hands for war, delivers David from the hurtful sword, and secures a flourishing, peaceful people whose God is the LORD.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 144:1", text:"Blessed be the LORD my strength, which teacheth my hands to war, and my fingers to fight:" },
    { ref:"Psalms 144:3", text:"LORD, what is man, that thou takest knowledge of him! or the son of man, that thou makest account of him!" },
    { ref:"Psalms 144:15", text:"Happy is that people, that is in such a case: yea, happy is that people, whose God is the LORD." }
  ],
  takeaway:"God trains, delivers, and provides: a people whose God is the LORD are truly happy.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What does the psalm say the LORD teaches the speaker's hands and fingers?", choices:["To harp and sing","To plant and harvest","To war and to fight","To write and to seal"], a:2, why:"Blessed be the LORD my strength, which teacheth my hands to war, and my fingers to fight." },
    { q:"How does the psalm measure man's life?", choices:["Strong as a high tower","Like vanity, his days as a shadow that passeth away","Enduring as polished corner stones","Numbered like the stars"], a:3, why:"Man is like to vanity: his days are as a shadow that passeth away — which makes God's taking knowledge of him amazing." },
    { q:"What picture of national well-being closes the psalm?", choices:["Sons and daughters flourishing, garners full, and no complaining in the streets","Conquest of every neighboring kingdom","Temples in every city","Tribute flowing from strangers"], a:0, why:"The vision runs from family to fields to streets: happy is that people, whose God is the LORD." }
  ] },
{ n:145, summary:"The great anthology of praise: this psalm gathers nearly every note the Psalter can sing. It begins personally — I will extol thee, my God, O king — promising praise every day and for ever and ever, with one generation praising God's works to another. The heart of the psalm is a portrait: great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised; his greatness unsearchable; gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger, and of great mercy; good to all, with tender mercies over all his works. His kingdom is everlasting, and his rule is practical: he upholdeth all that fall, raiseth up all that are bowed down, opens his hand to satisfy every living thing, and is nigh unto all them that call upon him in truth. The psalm's last line spreads the invitation wide: let all flesh bless his holy name for ever and ever.",
  context:"A comprehensive praise psalm blessing God's name daily across generations, praising his unsearchable greatness, everlasting kingdom, provision for every living thing, and nearness to all who call on him in truth.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 145:3", text:"Great is the LORD, and greatly to be praised; and his greatness is unsearchable." },
    { ref:"Psalms 145:8", text:"The LORD is gracious, and full of compassion; slow to anger, and of great mercy." },
    { ref:"Psalms 145:18", text:"The LORD is nigh unto all them that call upon him, to all that call upon him in truth." }
  ],
  takeaway:"The Lord is near to everyone who calls on him in truth, and all flesh is invited to bless his holy name.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"According to the psalm, how often will the singer bless the Lord?", choices:["Once a week at worship","Every day","Only at festival seasons","Seven times daily"], a:1, why:"Every day will I bless thee; and I will praise thy name for ever and ever." },
    { q:"How does the psalm describe God's care for those who stumble?", choices:["He watches them fall from far away","He demands they rise by themselves","He upholdeth all that fall, and raiseth up all those that be bowed down","He replaces them with the strong"], a:2, why:"The LORD upholdeth all that fall, and raiseth up all those that be bowed down — mercy built into his rule." },
    { q:"To whom is the Lord near, according to this psalm?", choices:["To the powerful and victorious","To those who never suffer","Only to Israel's priests","To all them that call upon him in truth"], a:3, why:"The LORD is nigh unto all them that call upon him, to all that call upon him in truth." }
  ] },
{ n:146, summary:"The closing hallel raises its first full voice here: praise the LORD, O my soul — while I live will I praise the LORD; I will sing praises unto my God while I have any being. The psalm's counsel follows: put not your trust in princes, nor in the son of man, in whom there is no help — a prince's breath goes forth, he returns to his earth, and in that very day his thoughts perish. The blessed alternative is the God of Jacob, who made heaven, earth, and sea and keepeth truth for ever. What that God does reads like a job description of mercy: he executeth judgment for the oppressed, giveth food to the hungry, looseth the prisoners, openeth the eyes of the blind, raiseth the bowed down, loveth the righteous, preserveth the strangers, relieveth the fatherless and widow — and turneth the way of the wicked upside down. The LORD shall reign for ever.",
  context:"A praise psalm contrasting failing princes with the God of Jacob, who keeps truth for ever and helps the oppressed, hungry, blind, stranger, fatherless, and widow while reigning for ever.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 146:3", text:"Put not your trust in princes, nor in the son of man, in whom there is no help." },
    { ref:"Psalms 146:5", text:"Happy is he that hath the God of Jacob for his help, whose hope is in the LORD his God:" },
    { ref:"Psalms 146:10", text:"The LORD shall reign for ever, even thy God, O Zion, unto all generations. Praise ye the LORD." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Trust belongs to the Maker of heaven and earth, not to princes whose plans die with them.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Whom does the psalm warn against trusting?", choices:["Princes and the son of man, in whom there is no help","Rich merchants and traders","Wise scribes and teachers","Armies and chariots"], a:0, why:"Put not your trust in princes... his breath goeth forth, he returneth to his earth; in that very day his thoughts perish." },
    { q:"What does the God of Jacob do for the vulnerable, according to the psalm?", choices:["Executes judgment for the oppressed, feeds the hungry, loosens prisoners, relieves fatherless and widow","Wages war against all foreigners","Distributes land equally every year","Rewrites the law for each generation"], a:1, why:"The catalog of mercy in verses 7-9 names the oppressed, hungry, prisoners, blind, bowed down, righteous, strangers, fatherless, and widow." },
    { q:"How long does the psalm say the LORD will reign?", choices:["Until the exile ends","For a thousand generations","For ever, even thy God, O Zion, unto all generations","As long as the temple stands"], a:2, why:"The LORD shall reign for ever, even thy God, O Zion, unto all generations — the ground of unshakable praise." }
  ] },
{ n:147, summary:"The God who counts stars also bandages hearts. Praise is comely, the psalm says, because of who the LORD is and what he does: he buildeth up Jerusalem and gathereth the outcasts of Israel; he healeth the broken in heart and bindeth up their wounds. Scale follows scale — he telleth the number of the stars and calleth them all by their names; his understanding is infinite — yet the same care reaches downward to weather and animals: clouds, rain, grass upon the mountains, food for the beasts and the young ravens which cry. Not strength but reverence draws him: he delighteth not in the strength of the horse, nor pleasure in the legs of a man; the LORD taketh pleasure in them that fear him, in those that hope in his mercy. Winter obeys his word — snow, hoarfrost, ice, then melting wind and flowing waters — and his statutes, given to Jacob and Israel, have been dealt to no other nation.",
  context:"A praise psalm pairing God's cosmic care — naming the stars, commanding snow and thaw — with his healing of the brokenhearted and his word given uniquely to Jacob and Israel.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 147:3", text:"He healeth the broken in heart, and bindeth up their wounds." },
    { ref:"Psalms 147:4", text:"He telleth the number of the stars; he calleth them all by their names." },
    { ref:"Psalms 147:19", text:"He sheweth his word unto Jacob, his statutes and his judgments unto Israel." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Infinite power and intimate care meet in the Lord: he names stars and binds up broken hearts.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What does the LORD do with the stars, according to the psalm?", choices:["Rearranges them each season","Borrows their light for the moon","Fights battles by their signs","Telleth the number of the stars and calleth them all by their names"], a:3, why:"He telleth the number of the stars; he calleth them all by their names — power joined to personal knowledge." },
    { q:"What does the LORD delight in, more than the strength of horses or the legs of men?", choices:["Them that fear him and hope in his mercy","Swift runners in the streets","Great armies of Jerusalem","Mountains full of cedars"], a:0, why:"The LORD taketh pleasure in them that fear him, in those that hope in his mercy." },
    { q:"What pair of ministries does the psalm give the LORD at its opening?", choices:["Judging and punishing","Building up Jerusalem and healing the broken in heart","Sowing and reaping","Ruling and taxing"], a:1, why:"He gathers the outcasts of Israel and healeth the broken in heart, binding up their wounds — restoration inside and out." }
  ] },
{ n:148, summary:"The universe is summoned to a choir. From the heavens first: all his angels, all his hosts, sun and moon, all ye stars of light, the heavens of heavens and the waters above the heavens. The reason for their praise is creation itself: for he commanded, and they were created; established for ever and ever by a decree that shall not pass. Then the summons drops to earth: dragons and all deeps, fire and hail, snow and vapour, stormy wind fulfilling his word, mountains and cedars, beasts and cattle, creeping things and flying fowl. Humanity files in last, from kings of the earth, princes, and judges to young men, maidens, old men, and children — for his name alone is excellent, his glory above the earth and heaven. The end names those nearest: the children of Israel, a people near unto him, whose praise God himself exalts.",
  context:"A creation-wide roll call of praise: angels, sun, moon, stars, weather, mountains, animals, kings, and children are all summoned to praise the name of the Lord, who made and established them.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 148:1", text:"Praise ye the LORD. Praise ye the LORD from the heavens: praise him in the heights." },
    { ref:"Psalms 148:5", text:"Let them praise the name of the LORD: for he commanded, and they were created." },
    { ref:"Psalms 148:14", text:"He also exalteth the horn of his people, the praise of all his saints; even of the children of Israel, a people near unto him." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Everything God made exists to praise him — and his people are nearest of all to the Maker they laud.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which group is listed LAST in the psalm's roll call of praisers?", choices:["Angels and heavenly hosts","Sun, moon, and stars","Kings of the earth and all people, young men and maidens, old men and children","Dragons and all deeps"], a:2, why:"The psalm moves from heavens to earth to humanity, ending with kings, people, and every age from maidens to children." },
    { q:"Why are the heavens able to praise the LORD, according to the psalm?", choices:["Because they sing without ceasing","For he commanded, and they were created","Because angels teach them songs","Because they reflect the saints' praise"], a:3, why:"Let them praise the name of the LORD: for he commanded, and they were created — creation owes praise to its Creator." },
    { q:"What does the psalm reveal about Israel's standing among all praisers?", choices:["A people near unto him, whose praise he exalts","Set apart to watch the others praise","Required to praise more than angels","Ordinary members of the choir"], a:0, why:"He exalteth the horn of his people... even of the children of Israel, a people near unto him." }
  ] },
{ n:149, summary:"A new song for a renewed people. The saints are summoned to sing unto the LORD a new song in the congregation, Israel to rejoice in him that made him, the children of Zion to be joyful in their King. The music is full-bodied — praise in the dance, the timbrel and the harp — and reaches even to the bedside: let them sing aloud upon their beds. The ground of the joy is personal: the LORD taketh pleasure in his people, and he will beautify the meek with salvation. Then the psalm arms the choir: let the high praises of God be in their mouth, and a twoedged sword in their hand, executing vengeance on the heathen, binding kings with chains and nobles with fetters of iron — the judgment written. This, the closing line insists, is the honour of all his saints.",
  context:"A victory-dance psalm: the saints sing a new song with timbrel, harp, and dance because the Lord delights in them, and carry high praises with a twoedged sword to execute the judgment written.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 149:1", text:"Praise ye the LORD. Sing unto the LORD a new song, and his praise in the congregation of saints." },
    { ref:"Psalms 149:4", text:"For the LORD taketh pleasure in his people: he will beautify the meek with salvation." },
    { ref:"Psalms 149:6", text:"Let the high praises of God be in their mouth, and a twoedged sword in their hand;" }
  ],
  takeaway:"God beautifies the meek with salvation; his people's praise and his justice travel together.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"What kind of song does the psalm call Israel to sing?", choices:["A song of the ages","A new song in the congregation of saints","An old song by the rivers","A midnight lament"], a:1, why:"Sing unto the LORD a new song, and his praise in the congregation of saints." },
    { q:"What does the LORD do for the meek, according to this psalm?", choices:["Gives them armies of their own","Teaches them the songs of Zion","Beautifies them with salvation","Clothes them in royal robes"], a:2, why:"For the LORD taketh pleasure in his people: he will beautify the meek with salvation." },
    { q:"What combination does the psalm place in the saints' hands and mouths?", choices:["Harps and wine cups","Incense and prayers","Scrolls and lamps","High praises of God in their mouth, and a twoedged sword in their hand"], a:3, why:"Worship and the execution of the judgment written go together — this honour have all his saints." }
  ] },
{ n:150, summary:"The Psalter ends where all its music was always headed: pure praise, with instruments named and reasons given. Praise God in his sanctuary; praise him in the firmament of his power — worship sounds from temple to sky. The reasons are stated plainly: praise him for his mighty acts, praise him according to his excellent greatness. Then comes the orchestra: the sound of the trumpet, psaltery and harp, timbrel and dance, stringed instruments and organs, loud cymbals and high sounding cymbals — every kind of sound pressed into service. The final verse widens the circle until it takes in all living things: let every thing that hath breath praise the LORD. The book of Psalms closes on the call it loves most: Praise ye the LORD.",
  context:"The final psalm of the Psalter: a six-verse fanfare calling for praise in the sanctuary and firmament, with trumpet, harp, timbrel, dance, and cymbals, until everything that hath breath praises the LORD.",
  keyVerses:[
    { ref:"Psalms 150:1", text:"Praise ye the LORD. Praise God in his sanctuary: praise him in the firmament of his power." },
    { ref:"Psalms 150:3", text:"Praise him with the sound of the trumpet: praise him with the psaltery and harp." },
    { ref:"Psalms 150:6", text:"Let every thing that hath breath praise the LORD. Praise ye the LORD." }
  ],
  takeaway:"Praise is the proper work of everything alive: every breath is an argument for praising the LORD.",
  quiz:[
    { q:"Which instruments does the psalm name for praising God?", choices:["Trumpet, psaltery, harp, timbrel, cymbals","Flute, drum, bells","Only the human voice","Lyre and lute only"], a:0, why:"The psalm lists the trumpet, psaltery and harp, timbrel and dance, stringed instruments and organs, and loud cymbals." },
    { q:"What two reasons does the psalm give for praising God?", choices:["Wealth and long life","His mighty acts and his excellent greatness","Mercy to Israel and defeat of Egypt","Creation and the flood"], a:1, why:"Praise him for his mighty acts: praise him according to his excellent greatness." },
    { q:"What qualifies a creature to join this final call to praise?", choices:["Mastery of an instrument","Living in Jerusalem","Having breath","Being without sin"], a:2, why:"Let every thing that hath breath praise the LORD — breath itself is the qualification." }
  ] }
);
